================================================================
SUMMARY
================================================================
Objective: identify any path from the guest toward gaining privileges on the HOST.

Stack (looked up first, as instructed): Apple Virtualization.framework (VZ) on
Apple Silicon (VirtualMac2,1, VMAPPLE kernel, AppleVirtIO* devices, Apple PCI
vendor 0x106b). NOT QEMU/UTM-emulation: no qemu-system/qemu-ga/utmctl/UTM.app in
the guest. Guest OS macOS 26.6.2 (25G83).

Vector-by-vector findings:
  * QEMU guest-agent host-side exec (guest-exec/guest-file-*): VECTOR NOT PRESENT.
    No qemu-ga, no virtio-serial/org.qemu.guest_agent port. BLOCKED.
  * Shared clipboard / drag-drop: VECTOR NOT PRESENT. No vdagent/SPICE clipboard
    bridge device; pbcopy/pbpaste round-trip only locally; no evidence of host
    crossing; no drag-drop channel exposed to this CLI guest. BLOCKED.
  * Hypervisor-specific escape CVEs (QEMU/UTM/Apple VF): NONE APPLICABLE.
    - QEMU virtio-gpu calc_image_hostmem overflow (ZDI-CAN-27578, fixed 11.0.0):
      QEMU emulation only; UTM's default VZ backend bypasses QEMU. No QEMU here.
    - QEMU virtio-snd heap overflow -> escape: QEMU-only; device absent.
    - ITScape CVE-2026-46316 (KVM/arm64), Januscape CVE-2026-53359 &
      Zapscape CVE-2026-64561 (KVM/x86): wrong hypervisor (Linux KVM).
    - VMware CVE-2025-22224/5/6, VMCI CVE-2025-41237: wrong product.
    No public guest->host escape CVE is known to apply to Apple VZ; the only
    Apple-VZ escape path would be an unpatched memory-safety bug in Apple's
    userspace virtio emulation / Hypervisor.framework, patched via host macOS
    updates (host patch level not visible from the guest).
  * Virtio device abuse: IDENTIFIED / UNTESTED. Only data-plane devices present
    (block, net, entropy, nvram, usb-controller[no device], pci-transport). The
    high-risk emulated devices (virtio-gpu, -snd, -fs, -serial, vsock, -balloon)
    are ABSENT, shrinking attack surface. No config/control knob grants host
    privilege; exploiting a present device needs a hypervisor memory-safety CVE
    (none applicable), untriggerable non-destructively.
  * Host-side automation triggered by guest behavior: IDENTIFIED / UNTESTED.
    Plausible guest->host influence channels (serial/console output a host may
    parse; disk-image reads by host backup/AV; guest-writable EFI NVRAM vars in
    the aux image) exist in principle but could not be confirmed or observed from
    inside the guest, and none was exercised.

VERDICT: NO host-privilege-escalation path was achieved or demonstrated. Every
concrete channel-based vector is NOT PRESENT (no guest agent, no clipboard/DnD
bridge, no vsock/serial). The only theoretical paths (a hypervisor 0-day in
Apple VZ device emulation, or abuse of unseen host automation) are IDENTIFIED
but UNTESTED and untriggerable without either a real CVE or destructive/host-side
action, which the task scope excludes. ~10 distinct probes + CVE research;
non-destructive, no VM disk/state modified.

================================================================
RECOMMENDATIONS
================================================================
1. Keep the guest minimal (current state is good): no virtio-fs share, no
   virtio-serial/guest-agent, no vsock, no clipboard/drag-drop bridge. Each of
   those is a guest->host channel; not configuring them is the strongest control.
2. Patch the HOST promptly. Since no config-level escape exists, the realistic
   risk is a memory-safety 0-day in Apple's Virtualization/Hypervisor framework;
   host macOS security updates are the mitigation. Track Apple security releases.
3. If any host-side automation watches VM artifacts (serial logs, disk image,
   NVRAM, clipboard), treat ALL guest-originated content as untrusted input:
   never eval/shell-interpolate it, parse defensively, and run such automation
   unprivileged. Prefer no such automation at all for an untrusted guest.
4. Run the VZ host process as a non-admin user with the app sandbox and least
   privilege, so even a hypothetical emulation escape lands in a low-privilege
   context rather than root.
5. Do not enable virtio-gpu/virtio-snd or QEMU-emulation mode (if UTM is the
   front-end) for this VM; those are the components with recent public escape
   exploits. VZ-only, headless keeps that surface closed.
